1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,821
Here's the reward for the last six lectures: twenty specific things to say, each one

2
00:00:04,821 --> 00:00:08,440
signalling, correctly, that you understand what you're asking about.

3
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:10,036
None of these are magic words.

4
00:00:10,036 --> 00:00:14,188
They work because you now know what's actually behind each one, the stack from

5
00:00:14,188 --> 00:00:18,552
lecture one, the retrieval mechanics, the difference between an average and a p95.

6
00:00:18,552 --> 00:00:23,023
Say any of these without that underneath, and the first follow-up question will find

7
00:00:23,023 --> 00:00:24,087
the gap immediately.

8
00:00:24,087 --> 00:00:28,398
Organised into four groups, matching the four things this section actually taught

9
00:00:28,398 --> 00:00:28,611
you.

10
00:00:28,611 --> 00:00:32,869
Read through once, then go back and notice how many of these are really the same

11
00:00:32,869 --> 00:00:37,073
move, applied to a different part of the stack: ask what's actually true before

12
00:00:37,073 --> 00:00:38,936
agreeing with what's being claimed.

13
00:00:39,486 --> 00:00:42,874
First group, straight from the stack diagram two lectures back.

14
00:00:42,874 --> 00:00:47,122
"Which layer is this actually in" is worth leading with in almost any debugging

15
00:00:47,122 --> 00:00:51,477
conversation, because it's the question that stops a vague complaint from staying

16
00:00:51,477 --> 00:00:51,800
vague.

17
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:56,102
"Retrieval or the model itself" does similar work specifically for anything that

18
00:00:56,102 --> 00:01:00,404
looks like a wrong answer, and it's usually the second question, right after the

19
00:01:00,404 --> 00:01:02,017
first one narrows things down.

20
00:01:02,017 --> 00:01:06,534
The fallback question is the one people skip under deadline pressure, and it's worth

21
00:01:06,534 --> 00:01:08,631
asking even when the answer is obvious.

22
00:01:08,631 --> 00:01:12,933
If nobody's thought about what happens when the call times out or errors, you've

23
00:01:12,933 --> 00:01:14,977
just found the gap before a user does.

24
00:01:14,977 --> 00:01:19,440
And asking prompt versus fine-tune, out loud, tells the room you know those are two

25
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:22,021
different costs, not two words for the same fix.

26
00:01:22,021 --> 00:01:24,387
Notice none of these need you to write code.

27
00:01:24,387 --> 00:01:28,850
They're technical in the sense of knowing where to point, not in the sense of being

28
00:01:28,850 --> 00:01:30,141
able to fix it yourself.

29
00:01:30,691 --> 00:01:35,237
Second group is everything from inside the RAG box, and "how are we chunking this"

30
00:01:35,237 --> 00:01:37,122
is the one worth memorising first.

31
00:01:37,122 --> 00:01:41,612
It's a question most PMs never think to ask, and it's very often where the actual

32
00:01:41,612 --> 00:01:44,883
bug lives: a badly split document, not a badly built model.

33
00:01:44,883 --> 00:01:47,822
Asking it early tells the room you already know that.

34
00:01:47,822 --> 00:01:52,257
"How fresh is the index" catches the other common failure, the confidently wrong

35
00:01:52,257 --> 00:01:55,029
answer built from something true eight months ago.

36
00:01:55,029 --> 00:01:59,630
And "is this grounded, or reasoning from memory" is worth asking any time an answer

37
00:01:59,630 --> 00:02:03,234
sounds suspiciously fluent for something nobody actually gave it.

38
00:02:03,234 --> 00:02:07,724
Fluent and grounded are not the same thing, and this question is how you find out

39
00:02:07,724 --> 00:02:09,277
which one you're looking at.

40
00:02:09,827 --> 00:02:14,445
Third group is where this section overlaps hardest with Section four, and "what's

41
00:02:14,445 --> 00:02:18,323
our pass rate on the golden set" is the one that does the most work.

42
00:02:18,323 --> 00:02:20,148
It presumes a golden set exists.

43
00:02:20,148 --> 00:02:24,880
Asking it plainly, as if of course there's one, is often the moment a room realises

44
00:02:24,880 --> 00:02:29,214
there isn't, and that's a far more useful outcome than the question sounding

45
00:02:29,214 --> 00:02:29,841
rhetorical.

46
00:02:29,841 --> 00:02:34,460
"Is that benchmark representative of our task" belongs right after anyone cites a

47
00:02:34,460 --> 00:02:36,798
leaderboard number in a model comparison.

48
00:02:36,798 --> 00:02:41,474
And "does that hold under real concurrent load" is the one question that catches a

49
00:02:41,474 --> 00:02:43,356
demo before it becomes a promise.

50
00:02:43,356 --> 00:02:46,834
Ask it before launch, not during the first busy Monday after.

51
00:02:47,384 --> 00:02:51,746
Last group is the one nobody enjoys asking, and it's the one that matters most.

52
00:02:51,746 --> 00:02:56,274
"What would make us turn this off" is rarely asked before launch, and it's exactly

53
00:02:56,274 --> 00:03:00,802
the disqualification checklist from Section one, asked one more time, out loud, in

54
00:03:00,802 --> 00:03:03,231
the room where it can still change the plan.

55
00:03:03,231 --> 00:03:07,372
"What's the blast radius" is worth asking about every feature, not just the

56
00:03:07,372 --> 00:03:11,790
risky-looking ones, because the answer tells you how much evaluation the feature

57
00:03:11,790 --> 00:03:12,783
actually owes you.

58
00:03:12,783 --> 00:03:17,256
And "how do we know it hasn't just memorised this" is the quiet, technical way of

59
00:03:17,256 --> 00:03:21,618
asking whether a benchmark score can be trusted at all, from two lectures back.

60
00:03:22,168 --> 00:03:25,278
Say the honest caveat, because this list is easy to misuse.

61
00:03:25,278 --> 00:03:29,601
Memorise the twenty phrases without the six lectures behind them, and you'll ask a

62
00:03:29,601 --> 00:03:32,448
sharp question and have nothing to do with the answer.

63
00:03:32,448 --> 00:03:36,454
That's worse than not asking, because it's now visible that the question was

64
00:03:36,454 --> 00:03:37,772
borrowed, not understood.

65
00:03:37,772 --> 00:03:42,200
Use these because you've earned them over this section, not instead of earning them.

66
00:03:42,750 --> 00:03:46,059
One thing before you move on, and keep it small on purpose.

67
00:03:46,059 --> 00:03:50,770
Pick three phrases from all twenty, the ones that fit a conversation you can already

68
00:03:50,770 --> 00:03:51,948
see coming this week.

69
00:03:51,948 --> 00:03:53,743
Twenty at once is a performance.

70
00:03:53,743 --> 00:03:55,650
Three, used naturally, is a habit.

71
00:03:55,650 --> 00:04:00,361
Say each one out loud, and imagine the actual follow-up question: can you answer it?

72
00:04:00,361 --> 00:04:02,997
If so, you're ready to use the phrase for real.

73
00:04:02,997 --> 00:04:05,128
That closes out the vocabulary itself.

74
00:04:05,128 --> 00:04:09,671
Next lecture recaps the section and hands off to Section three, where you finally

75
00:04:09,671 --> 00:04:11,971
write a spec an engineer actually trusts.
